INT. PIGEON-RACING CLUB - BANQUET HALL - EVENING

A low-ceilinged hall that smells of boiled ham and floor wax. Trophies line the walls, each topped with a small bronze bird in mid-flap. Round tables wear paper cloths. One table, at the back, has been set for three, and a single basket of bread rolls sits between the places like a negotiated settlement.

OTTOLINE VANDERMEER (60s) arrives first, in a green velvet dress she has plainly owned since the club's previous presidency. She arranges the cutlery by the width of a thumb, sits, and begins to examine her roll as if it might be called to testify.

BARNABY CROWE (50s) enters carrying a wrapped trophy under one arm and a plastic bag of seed under the other, which he has not been able to leave in the coat room. He stands for a moment at the end of the table. He sets the trophy down gently, face-forward. He sets the seed down with considerably less ceremony.

CLEMENTINE ASHBY-NOLAN (30s), the youngest person in the room by twenty years, comes in last. She has a clipboard. She also has the expression of someone who has already lost the argument she is about to start.

OTTOLINE
You're sitting there?

BARNABY
It was my chair last year.

OTTOLINE
Last year you were also winning.

BARNABY
I'm winning this year.

CLEMENTINE
The scores aren't posted until after the soup.

OTTOLINE
Then you can sit where you like until after the soup.

Barnaby does not sit. He lifts the trophy by its base and sets it on the table, an inch closer to Ottoline's plate than to his own.

BARNABY
I wrapped it myself.

OTTOLINE
It looks wrapped.

BARNABY
Four layers.

OTTOLINE
In what?

BARNABY
In tissue. Mostly.

OTTOLINE
Mostly.

CLEMENTINE
(consulting clipboard)
It's, ah. It's the Hartley Cup. Which is, strictly, for the best bird in the northern loft.

BARNABY
It is.

OTTOLINE
And Barnaby's northern loft is where?

BARNABY
Right where it's always been.

OTTOLINE
Behind a dry cleaner's.

BARNABY
It's a very good dry cleaner's.

CLEMENTINE
I think that's not the question.

OTTOLINE
No. The question is whether it's a very good bird.

BARNABY
She flew four hundred miles.

OTTOLINE
So I'm told.

BARNABY
In nine hours.

OTTOLINE
In nine hours, from a basket, in a van, according to the van driver.

CLEMENTINE
I have the van driver's log. If anyone wants it.

BARNABY
Nobody wants it.

OTTOLINE
I'd like to see it.

Clementine slides the clipboard across the table. Barnaby's hand lands on it first. For a moment all three hands rest on the same sheet of paper, and none of them moves.

BARNABY
It's the club's business.

OTTOLINE
I am the club's business. Fifty-one years.

CLEMENTINE
You were treasurer for thirty of them.

OTTOLINE
Thirty-one.

CLEMENTINE
I stand corrected.

BARNABY
You don't stand at all. You sit with a clipboard.

CLEMENTINE
It's a very good clipboard.

OTTOLINE
(to her roll)
Everything in this room is very good.

A waiter passes with soup. All three lean back, hands retreating from the clipboard, and watch him set down three bowls. Nobody reaches for a spoon.

OTTOLINE (CONT'D)
Is it mushroom?

BARNABY
It's brown.

CLEMENTINE
It's mushroom. I checked.

OTTOLINE
You check everything.

CLEMENTINE
Someone has to.

BARNABY
You could have checked the bird.

CLEMENTINE
I did.

Pause.

OTTOLINE
And?

CLEMENTINE
And she's a very good bird.

BARNABY
Thank you.

CLEMENTINE
It's not the same bird that was entered.

A spoon is lifted. A spoon is set down. Barnaby slides the trophy back an inch toward himself, then, looking at no one, an inch toward the middle of the table.

OTTOLINE
I'd like to see her.

BARNABY
She's in the car.

OTTOLINE
In the car.

BARNABY
In the basket. On the back seat. With the windows down an inch.

OTTOLINE
Fetch her.

BARNABY
It's raining.

OTTOLINE
She's a pigeon.

BARNABY
She's nervous.

CLEMENTINE
(gently)
Mr. Crowe. Which bird is she?

Barnaby opens his mouth. He closes it again. He picks up his spoon and tastes the soup, and his face does something complicated, and he tastes it again.

BARNABY
It is mushroom.

OTTOLINE
It is.

BARNABY
It's actually quite good.

OTTOLINE
It always has been.

She tears her roll in half. She puts one half on Barnaby's bread plate, and does not look at him while she does it.

CLEMENTINE
So the scores?

OTTOLINE
After the soup.
